  "platform": "Mega Drive",
  "summary": "Nigel’s search for King Nole’s treasure turns isometric rooms into puzzles of height, movement and perspective.",
  "body": [
    "Landstalker is Climax Entertainment’s action adventure for Mega Drive, first released in Japan on 30 October 1992. Nigel, a treasure hunter, travels with the fairy Friday in search of the riches of King Nole. Towns, outdoor paths and dungeons all use an isometric view, giving the journey a consistent sense of height and depth. Sword fighting shares the same space with jumping, conversations and movable objects. Progress comes from understanding how those everyday actions fit the room in front of Nigel, as well as defeating its monsters.",
    "A platform may appear close to another while sitting at a different height, so judging a jump becomes a small spatial puzzle. Blocks can provide steps, switches can open a route, and a locked door may require a detour before Nigel returns with its key. The same diagonal view also frames combat, making position important when approaching an enemy with the sword. Rather than separating puzzles into a different screen, the game places monsters, traps and routes together inside spaces the player has to read carefully.",
    "Nigel starts with a compact set of actions: walking, jumping, swinging his sword, climbing and lifting objects. New equipment improves his strength and protection, while items help him survive or open the next part of the adventure. Because those basic movements remain familiar, later rooms can ask more demanding questions of the same skills. The journey also returns to earlier towns and dungeons, turning remembered places into useful landmarks. Motoaki Takenouchi’s score accompanies this treasure hunt through a world built from interlocking rooms and paths."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Nigel and the treasure",
    "Finding the height of a jump",
    "Tools for the next room"
  ],
